1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,920
How does a merchant give money back?

2
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:03,960
A buyer returns a pair of shoes.

3
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:06,624
Now picture three things going wrong.

4
00:00:06,674 --> 00:00:09,168
The merchant presses refund twice.

5
00:00:09,218 --> 00:00:13,440
The bank's support team refunds the same payment again over the phone.

6
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:17,856
And a refund made six months later reaches a card that no longer exists.

7
00:00:17,906 --> 00:00:20,712
Each mistake pays out too much, or too late.

8
00:00:20,762 --> 00:00:22,968
Go back to the tab book from the second lesson.

9
00:00:23,018 --> 00:00:27,240
Before the day closes, the shopkeeper simply crosses the line out.

10
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:29,904
That is a reversal, and it costs nothing.

11
00:00:29,954 --> 00:00:33,408
After the day closes, the shopkeeper hands the money back.

12
00:00:33,458 --> 00:00:35,280
That is a refund.

13
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:37,488
The day decides which one happens.

14
00:00:37,538 --> 00:00:40,224
The day is counted in the bank's own time zone.

15
00:00:40,274 --> 00:00:43,344
And the whole amount always goes back, never a part.

16
00:00:43,394 --> 00:00:48,000
The merchant can give back a paid payment by itself, but only up to its refund cap.

17
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:52,464
That is the bank's chosen largest amount a merchant may give back on its own.

18
00:00:52,514 --> 00:00:56,688
A later refund must also fall inside the refund window.

19
00:00:56,738 --> 00:01:00,576
That is the bank's chosen number of working days after the payment.

20
00:01:00,626 --> 00:01:04,368
Two bank admins together can make one website's window longer.

21
00:01:04,418 --> 00:01:08,016
Inside these limits, giving back works like the charge.

22
00:01:08,066 --> 00:01:12,264
One claim lets only one caller win, however many clicks arrive.

23
00:01:12,314 --> 00:01:16,584
A line is written in the bank's logbook first, with the merchant's reason.

24
00:01:16,634 --> 00:01:21,024
Then ecom asks the card zone, by token, with the call's own id.

25
00:01:21,074 --> 00:01:22,848
Approved is final.

26
00:01:22,898 --> 00:01:27,744
Refused leaves the payment paid, and the merchant is told the refund failed.

27
00:01:27,794 --> 00:01:33,960
No answer is asked about later, the same way as a silent charge, and never sent twice.

28
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:39,144
Beyond the cap, or after the window, the merchant cannot give money back alone.

29
00:01:39,194 --> 00:01:41,016
The bank does it instead.

30
00:01:41,066 --> 00:01:43,104
That is a refund by application.

31
00:01:43,154 --> 00:01:44,976
The merchant writes to the bank.

32
00:01:45,026 --> 00:01:49,008
One bank admin asks for the refund, and a second admin approves it.

33
00:01:49,058 --> 00:01:50,880
Now watch the product.

34
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:53,592
In the merchant portal, open a paid payment.

35
00:01:53,642 --> 00:01:58,608
A box says refundable until, with the date, and how many days are left.

36
00:01:58,658 --> 00:02:00,480
Press give back.

37
00:02:00,530 --> 00:02:02,904
A window asks: give the money back?

38
00:02:02,954 --> 00:02:08,232
It explains that the whole amount goes back to the buyer's card, reversed today, or refunded later.

39
00:02:08,282 --> 00:02:12,288
It asks for a reason, which both the merchant and the bank will see.

40
00:02:12,338 --> 00:02:14,160
Confirm it.

41
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:16,632
The status turns to giving the money back.

42
00:02:16,682 --> 00:02:20,712
Moments later it says reversed, because it is still the same day.

43
00:02:20,762 --> 00:02:25,152
Under calls to the card scheme, a reversal line appears below the debit.

44
00:02:25,202 --> 00:02:28,104
On the buyer's page, it says money returned.

45
00:02:28,154 --> 00:02:30,864
Now open a large payment, above the cap.

46
00:02:30,914 --> 00:02:32,568
There is no give back button.

47
00:02:32,618 --> 00:02:39,480
A grey note says above the refund limit, and that larger refunds are made by the bank on a written application.

48
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:41,352
Open an old payment.

49
00:02:41,402 --> 00:02:46,968
The note says the refund window has closed, and gives the date it could be refunded until.

50
00:02:47,018 --> 00:02:50,304
A teammate who only views sees no refund box at all.

51
00:02:50,354 --> 00:02:52,536
Now switch to the bank's admin portal.

52
00:02:52,586 --> 00:02:56,952
An admin opens that old payment and chooses refund by application.

53
00:02:57,002 --> 00:03:00,216
They enter the application number and a note saying why.

54
00:03:00,266 --> 00:03:04,632
They press send for approval, and the screen says it was sent to another admin.

55
00:03:04,682 --> 00:03:08,256
Only when a second admin approves does the money go back.

56
00:03:08,306 --> 00:03:10,128
What does this protect?

57
00:03:10,178 --> 00:03:12,360
The claim stops double refunds.

58
00:03:12,410 --> 00:03:15,720
The logbook means every refund can be explained.

59
00:03:15,770 --> 00:03:20,280
The cap and the window protect the merchant from large or late mistakes.

60
00:03:20,330 --> 00:03:24,360
And two admins protect the bank from one person moving money alone.

61
00:03:24,410 --> 00:03:25,920
So back to the tab book.

62
00:03:25,970 --> 00:03:28,440
Cross it out today, or hand it back later.

63
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:32,280
Once, written down first, and within the shop's rules.

64
00:03:32,330 --> 00:03:35,496
Next: following one payment through the whole system.
